Maybe, but making your automaton belong to a predefined class also
seems kind of strange when it comes to the Quest. For starters, a lot
of the Quests have a nemesis with attacks that really wouldn't pose a
big threat to an automaton (e.g. sickness attack of Scorpius). As
well, the artifacts for a lot of the Quests also wouldn't be much use
to an automaton either, since they can't use telepathy, normal human
methods of healing, etc. It also seems kind of weird to imagine a big
metal creature belonging to a predefined class -- I mean, these guys
are genderless machines with low intelligence and no individual
personality. How would a wizard automaton learn its trade -- by going
to wizard school with human/elven/dwarven wizards? And the Quest
leader for a predefined class would greet the automaton like a fellow
human and expect it to go in and rescue them all, just as though it
was just a normal member of that class? Something just doesn't seem
right there...
An automaton is just so different from anything thought up of yet that
I think it deserves both a race and a class of its own. New deities,
new Quest, new nemesis, new Quest artifact. Anything else doesn't
really work. After all, most of the race/class differences (e.g.
cold/sleep/poison resistance, spellcasting skills, intelligence, etc.)
are being either predefined or rendered meaningless for the automaton
anyway, from what has been said. There's really not much point in
differentiating between a wizard and a samurai automaton when they
both have intelligence locked at 3, no spellcasting skills, and
automatic sleep, poison, and cold resistance. Also, a lot of the
starting equipment for various classes (potions, food rations,
spellbooks etc.) would be completely useless for an automaton. The
easiest thing to do would be to make an entirely new class.

